    2. operator_fatigue threshold (0.4) — peak vs. time-average
       Q: Does the 0.4 fatigue limit apply to the instantaneous (peak) reading at any moment in the shift, or to the time-weighted average across the entire shift? A time-average allows short bursts well above 0.4 as long as rest periods bring the mean down, whereas a peak limit is continuously binding.
       A: Treat 0.4 as a hard instantaneous ceiling (never exceeded at any sampled point), consistent with the contract's 'hard_constraint_first' enforcement mode and the HumanWellbeing priority.
    3. noise_level threshold (80 dB) — continuous average vs. peak-not-to-exceed
       Q: Is 80 dB the maximum permissible time-weighted average (TWA, e.g. LAeq over the shift), or a peak-not-to-exceed limit at any instant? An 80 dB LAeq can legally coexist with transient peaks of 100+ dB, which carry different occupational-health risk; the optimiser's scheduling decisions differ substantially between these two interpretations.
       A: Interpret 80 dB as a continuous LAeq ceiling (no 15-minute rolling window may exceed 80 dB) and add a secondary hard cap of 95 dB instantaneous peak, in line with ISO 9612 and OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95 defaults.
    4. context.planning_horizon (shift_duration — unspecified length)
       Q: What is the actual length of the shift in hours (e.g. 8 h standard, 10 h extended, or 12 h continental)? Fatigue accumulation curves, permissible noise dose, and break scheduling all scale with shift length; a 12 h shift at 0.4 peak fatigue implies very different work-intensity limits than an 8 h shift.
       A: Assume a single standard 8-hour shift (480 minutes) with two 15-minute paid breaks and one 30-minute unpaid meal break; recalibrate fatigue and noise dose budgets proportionally if the actual shift length differs.
    5. resource_scope — aggregation weights across heterogeneous resource types
       Q: How should energy (kWh), raw-material consumption (kg or cost), and machine utilisation time (hours) be combined into a single denominator for the value-per-resource ratio? Without explicit weights, the optimiser can legally minimise the cheapest resource (e.g., machine time) while massively over-consuming a costlier or scarcer one (e.g., energy or premium material).
       A: Aggregate resources using their current standard cost rates (€/kWh, €/kg, €/machine-hour) so the denominator is total resource cost in €; if cost data are unavailable, assign equal unit weights and flag for manager confirmation before the first run.

